\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{Regime Transition Mechanism.}
At $K{=}3$, a majority requires $t{=}2$; a single compromised judge flips the panel, making $\eta_{\max}$ the binding constraint. At $K{\geq}5$, the adversary must compromise ${\geq}3$ judges, and high-$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ panels present more distinct attack surfaces. The transition threshold $K^*$ lies between 3 and 5.

\paragraph{Attack-Mechanism-Dependent Structure.}
The aggregate positive $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ coefficient masks a 6:6 directional split at $K{=}3$ (11/12 consistent at $K{=}5$; Appendix~\ref{app:bft_reversal}). Targeted attacks are consistent with judge-specific vulnerabilities amplified by independence; shared-bias attacks are consistent with common biases diluted by diversity. Unconditional claims about diversity being ``harmful'' or ``beneficial'' are thus misleading.

\paragraph{Diversity--Capability Confound.}
$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ are correlated at $r{=}0.70$ ($K{=}3$) and $r{=}0.81$ ($K{=}5$; Figure~\ref{fig:keff_eta_scatter}), reflecting a structural property: maximizing diversity requires sampling across capability tiers, introducing weaker judges (Appendix~\ref{app:diversity_capability}). Cinelli--Hazlett sensitivity analysis \citep{cinelli2020making} shows the known $\eta_{\max}$ confound exceeds $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s robustness value in 28/36 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity}), with robustness increasing with $K$ (1/12 $\to$ 3/12 $\to$ 4/12). The API-only subset ($r{=}0.85$) does not replicate the transition (3/12). Capability-matched subsampling ($r{=}0.50$, 9 models, 126 panels at $K{=}5$; Figure~\ref{fig:keff_eta_scatter}, orange) provides partial mitigation: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ strengthens to 9/12 and RV exceeds $\eta_{\max}$ in 6/12 conditions (conservatively 5/12; Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity_poolA}), versus 3/12 in the full pool. The transition is thus a descriptive regime shift whose causal attribution to diversity requires capability-controlled designs.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_keff_eta_scatter.pdf}

\label{app:robustness_checks}

Table~\ref{tab:robustness_checks} summarizes regression results across model subsets and alternative specifications at $K{=}5$, testing whether the regime transition is robust to collinearity reduction and specification choice.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Subset / Method} & $\boldsymbol{r}$ & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ & \textbf{Trans.} \\
\midrule
Full bootstrap       & 0.70 & 0/12 & 7/12 & \checkmark \\
API-only (9) clust.  & 0.85 & 8/12 & 3/12 & --- \\
Cap-matched A (5A+4L) & 0.50 & ---  & 9/12 & \checkmark\checkmark \\
Cap-matched B (6A+3L) & 0.73 & ---  & 2/12 & --- \\
Fractional logit     & 0.70 & 5/12 & 9/12 & \checkmark \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Regime transition robustness at $K{=}5$ across model subsets and specifications. $r$: correlation between $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$. $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ columns show FDR-significant counts out of 12 conditions. Trans.: whether $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominates $\eta_{\max}$. API-only models ($r{=}0.85$, VIF${=}4.4$) show partially collinear predictors; capability-matched subsamples ($r{=}0.50$) strengthen the transition. Fractional logit \citep{papke1996econometric} confirms OLS patterns with 93.1\% directional agreement.}
\label{tab:robustness_checks}
\end{table}

The API-only subset ($r{=}0.85$, VIF${=}4.4$) demonstrates that elevated collinearity between $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ partially inflates standard errors: both predictors achieve moderate significance but neither dominates, and the transition does not replicate. Breaking collinearity through capability-matched subsampling (pool~A, $r{=}0.50$) strengthens $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s emergence to 9/12, exceeding the full-pool 7/12. Pool~B ($r{=}0.73$) falls near the decorrelation threshold, and the transition weakens accordingly. Random 9-model subsampling (1{,}000 trials) confirms an inverted-U: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominance rate peaks when the subsample includes 3--4 local models (maximizing capability spread) and drops when the pool is too homogeneous (Appendix~\ref{app:random_subsample}).

\section{Random Subsample Analysis}

